#!/bin/sh
#
# imprint - commit-msg gate (TypeSafe Jev commit-message & diff check)
#
# WHAT IT ENFORCES. Opt-in and fail-open: if `ts-commit-check` is not on PATH,
# this hook exits 0 and checks nothing. When it is on PATH, it runs
# `ts-commit-check --msg-file "$1"`, which runs its own local check first,
# needing no TypeSafe API key: a secret, API key, password, or email-shaped
# string in the commit message, in the newly added diff lines, or in a changed
# file name sets its leak probability to 1.0, and that alone refuses the
# commit (exit 1). For a merge (MERGE_HEAD present) the diff lines are only
# those new against every parent, and the file names only paths no parent has;
# docs/commit-msg-hook.md says exactly what is read. With a key configured,
# `ts-commit-check` also asks TypeSafe System One (Jev) to judge how well the
# message matches the diff (alignment), whether it omits significant changes,
# and whether it follows Conventional Commits style - but in this hook's plain
# `--msg-file` mode (no `--strict`), only the leak check can refuse the commit;
# the other three print in the report and let it through either way.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT ENFORCE, which is the larger half here too. Alignment,
# omissions and Conventional Commits style stay advisory in this hook:
# `ts-commit-check --strict`, run by hand or from CI, turns the first two into
# blockers as well, but this hook does not pass that flag.
#
# INSTALL - per clone, and it does not arrive with one (the same line
# `.githooks/pre-push` needs; one `core.hooksPath` covers both files):
#     git config core.hooksPath .githooks
# `ts-commit-check` itself is not shipped on this repository's PATH - see
# tools/typesafe/README.md for building or installing it.
#
# BYPASS. `git commit --no-verify` skips this hook silently, the same as it
# does for pre-push, and this script cannot see that it happened.
#
# FAIL-OPEN BY DESIGN - unlike pre-push's fail-closed gate. A missing
# `ts-commit-check` binary is not treated as a failure here, it is the
# opted-out state, so the hook exits 0 instead of refusing the commit.
#
# In the repository's own four-state vocabulary: the local leak check is
# **enforced** whenever `ts-commit-check` is on PATH; alignment, omissions and
# Conventional Commits style are **behaviour rules** in this hook's mode, with
# `--strict` elsewhere as the enforceable-but-not-enforced-here tooling; and
# whether `ts-commit-check` is installed at all is reserved to each clone.

command -v ts-commit-check >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0
exec ts-commit-check --msg-file "$1"
